<page type="text">
  <layout>One page of text: a four-line italic verse epigram set centred in the upper-middle area, followed by the initials "G. B." in bold, set right of centre. No margin notes. A dark decorative rule runs across the top edge (not text).</layout>
  <page_number></page_number>
  <running_head></running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <p><i>Occulta cui Mentis mala</i><br/><i>Secreta rodunt pectora,</i><br/><i>Huic est molesta Veritas</i><br/><i>Quae cor revelat turbidum.</i></p>
    <p><b>G. B.</b></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>The first letters of the verse lines are partly obscured at the left edge; "Secreta" and "Huic" are read with some uncertainty ("Secreta" appears as "ecreta" with a damaged initial, "Huic" as "Iuic"). The "e" in "est" is printed as a long-s form "eſt" in the original; rendered here as plain s. Page number and catchword absent.</notes>
</page>